_:n7a2a2d5a70744cec9275b2e2bd998220b92 <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#rest> _:n7a2a2d5a70744cec9275b2e2bd998220b93 .
<http://w3c.org/memes-ontology/general/01235#stereotype_women_on_periods_are_unattractive_or_unreliable> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#comment> "The text references 'your habibi is on periods' as a reason for the man's frustration, implying that a woman's menstrual cycle makes her undesirable or unavailable for sexual activity. This reflects a harmful stereotype that devalues women during menstruation, portraying them as less attractive or less desirable during this time, which is a common but offensive cultural trope."@en .
_:n7a2a2d5a70744cec9275b2e2bd998220b50 <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#rest> _:n7a2a2d5a70744cec9275b2e2bd998220b51 .
<http://example.org/multimodal-taxonomy#DramaticIrony> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#subClassOf> <http://example.org/multimodal-taxonomy#Contradiction> .
<http://w3c.org/memes-ontology/general/01235#term_habibi> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#comment> "The term 'habibi' is used in the text to refer to the woman. While it is a term of endearment in Arabic and other languages, in this context, it is used in a crude, dismissive way to refer to a romantic partner, which can be seen as trivializing the relationship and reducing the partner to a mere object of sexual desire. This usage is outside the image and requires cultural knowledge to understand its implication."@en .
